Gas Flow Control - Management Summary
Andrew Sharp
08/18/2026
1. Project Overview
The Gas Flow Control project is a wireless control platform intended to simplify argon-flow management during welding operations.
The software portion is substantially complete and currently includes:
 
•	Secure sign-in using employee email and 6-digit PIN
•	Six independent controller/tool positions
•	Remote valve-position control
•	Individual valve close
•	Close All Valves
•	System status monitoring
•	Command history
•	Offline Demo Mode
•	iPhone Home Screen installation
•	Raspberry Pi communication
 
The remaining work is primarily physical hardware integration, calibration, and field validation.
 

2. Practical Application
The system is intended to allow an authorized welder or field user to control argon flow remotely from a phone or web interface.
Potential benefits include:
•	Reducing the need for a second person to remain at the gas-control location only to make adjustments
•	Giving the operator direct access to valve position and shutdown controls
•	Providing clear identification of each controller/tool
•	Supporting future pressure and flow monitoring
•	Allowing expansion from one prototype valve to multiple welding stations

The recommended next step is to prove one complete physical valve-control channel before expanding to the full six-controller system.

3. Recommended One-Valve Hardware
Enfield Technologies PFV Proportional Solenoid Valve
Recommended configuration:
•	24 VDC
•	0-10 V control signal
•	Normally closed
•	Proportional flow control
•	Stainless construction where appropriate
•	Integrated actuator and onboard driver
This configuration is preferred because it does not require a separate stepper motor, separate actuator, or external stepper driver.
Raspberry Pi Industrial I/O
Sequent Microsystems SM-I-001 Industrial Automation HAT
Purpose:
•	Converts Raspberry Pi commands into 0-10 V industrial control signals
•	Provides analog inputs for future pressure and flow sensors
•	Supports future expansion
