4. Estimated One-Valve Prototype Cost
Item	Estimated Cost
Raspberry Pi 4 Model B	Already owned
Sequent SM-I-001 Industrial Automation HAT	~$120
Enfield PFV proportional valve	~$250-$275
24 VDC DIN-rail power supply	~$20-$30
DIN rail, terminals, fuse protection	~$30-$60
Control wire, ferrules, labels, misc. wiring	~$20-$40
Prototype enclosure/mounting hardware	~$25-$50
Estimated total	~$465-$575
Recommended Initial Budget
Approximately $500-$600
This estimate does not include:
•	Argon flow sensor
•	Pressure transducer
•	Final gas tubing/fittings
•	Filter
•	Shipping/tax
These items should be selected only after the field requirements are confirmed.
________________________________________
5. Questions That Still Need to Be Answered
Before ordering the exact valve, pressure sensor, and flow sensor, the field team needs to confirm:
•	What is the normal argon flow rate in CFH?
•	What is the minimum argon flow required?
•	What is the maximum argon flow required?
•	What is the regulated pressure entering the control valve in PSI?
•	What regulator/flowmeter is currently being used?
•	What tubing/fitting size is currently used?
•	Where will the controller and valve be physically mounted?
•	Will the equipment be exposed to dust, moisture, heat, grinding debris, or impact?
•	What should the valve do if power is lost?
•	What manual gas-isolation method will remain available?
These answers will determine the exact valve orifice, sensor ranges, fittings, and final enclosure requirements.
________________________________________
6. Items Not Needed for the First Prototype
Using the Enfield PFV with onboard driver means the following are not required for the first prototype:
•	Separate stepper motor
•	NEMA 17 motor
•	A4988 or DRV8825 driver
•	Enfield D5 stepper driver
•	Separate actuator
•	Second Raspberry Pi
•	Six valves
•	Six complete controller assemblies
•	Additional UI development
The goal is to validate one channel first, then scale only after successful testing.
________________________________________
7. First Prototype Goal
The first physical milestone is:
Gas Flow Control App
→ Raspberry Pi
→ Industrial 0-10 V I/O
→ Enfield PFV Valve
→ Physical valve response
Initial electrical verification:
•	0% command ≈ 0.0 V
•	25% command ≈ 2.5 V
•	50% command ≈ 5.0 V
•	75% command ≈ 7.5 V
•	100% command ≈ 10.0 V
After this is verified, the next steps are:
•	Add real pressure sensing
•	Add real argon flow sensing
•	Perform calibration
•	Conduct controlled gas testing
•	Begin field validation
________________________________________
8. Gas Flow Control Web App
Live Application:
https://gas-flow-control-demo.web.app
The app can be opened in Safari and added directly to an iPhone Home Screen.
The current Demo Mode can operate without an Internet connection after the application has been installed and cached.
________________________________________
9. Management Recommendation
Proceed with a single-valve prototype before committing to a full six-valve system.
Recommended Initial Approval
$500-$600
This amount is enough to prove the core electrical and valve-control concept while minimizing project cost and risk.
The exact proportional valve configuration, pressure sensor, flow sensor, and gas-side fittings should be finalized only after the remaining field pressure and CFH requirements are confirmed.

